#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555891} :activity/id #uuid "8204430f-7743-5e1b-97bd-65f00f3fe35a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048493 :topic/id #uuid "a45cb440-1b11-5eea-ac7d-09ef4b31794b" :topic/math-academy-id 3335} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555883 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492002 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38e29233-e0dc-59a9-8b71-89547fc06293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491564} {:answer/id #uuid "4e8be98c-dbfa-5fb0-9905-3ffacd2e675f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} & \\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491601} {:answer/id #uuid "9cbc487c-5094-5d5f-8c80-f2cbf38f3c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491728} {:answer/id #uuid "a2cf2940-8c8f-50ee-bc73-4ae0c1be7870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} & -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491740} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb57498-4f30-5829-bbc1-a63f82ea49d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491826}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491826} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc785a6c-6e3c-5d7b-a2af-c97237d81ba3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "23817e7f-6a06-51ca-bb89-ae0fdb8c7004" :question/math-academy-id "q-94049" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "157496fd-75d6-56b5-bfa4-94bcbd0776e1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555908}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555891 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172613 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6386cba4-6bef-5ce6-a28f-7756865697af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555222} {:answer/id #uuid "87b92cc8-999d-5cf3-bf8b-bdd8e08a2989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555316} {:answer/id #uuid "88323906-807d-5c80-975d-3c3a60ac5954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555318} {:answer/id #uuid "a5853336-65bd-51a4-8d09-1814205711bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555395} {:answer/id #uuid "c5ca4907-7b6a-5cd0-ad7e-2e9a14158a1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555478}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555222} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebf95aab-26ad-5d4c-96ff-37b37822372e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c7e02b11-9226-5c5b-9fda-bf26ea5fa1e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-93602" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=10.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=5\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=10$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "32350c25-225c-5d5b-849f-e0a560c38b8d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555948}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357631 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1383bfc2-4360-5709-a0a8-f10b27226553" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354836} {:answer/id #uuid "1cf62f53-4cca-53fd-9904-3006d9e5e4c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354914} {:answer/id #uuid "6c84ca9d-2a09-535e-ac82-9783439e70e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355622} {:answer/id #uuid "8f61224e-9396-5066-9c8e-bd0d738416d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355931} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c07977-b2ba-5f8d-bb10-ef441705d5a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356382}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355931} :answer-field/id #uuid "110fa06e-fdc4-596b-8460-688e417bf995" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5fd6de92-b207-5bcd-bc32-b23f271f69c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94039" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=8$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ Computing $A-(-5)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{bmatrix}+5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{13}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "546364d3-3227-5aec-9b6e-8dc453cb8430" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555883}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555908 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555870 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a706c372-7bf9-519b-bf47-eec640055a09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555398} {:answer/id #uuid "c0eaea37-d763-51e1-b779-86ad5cab32fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555463} {:answer/id #uuid "d5816e57-b424-539b-bcae-e9c26275321d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555507} {:answer/id #uuid "f73191f0-64c8-52a9-bb54-a9dff6856396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555612} {:answer/id #uuid "fc03ffd9-3aab-5745-80b2-933e00a5c3a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555627}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555627} :answer-field/id #uuid "6125238e-cd95-58be-8f9d-65d56d196e45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f4374452-85e0-5d4e-85af-02cf21a24ee9" :question/math-academy-id "q-94051" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=9$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-8.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=9\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-8$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-9I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{aligned}]-9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-8I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-8)I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{aligned}]+8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-8)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "589cc2a9-3241-538e-b04a-719092fbc24c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555948 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172615 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1afbd007-f1e6-5e55-8cbe-4bcfee30c365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491507} {:answer/id #uuid "4a38eb59-71d6-57ae-b362-18321880abad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491595} {:answer/id #uuid "6b57765b-b3fc-58da-ae16-8958c804d7b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491646} {:answer/id #uuid "6c66f93f-55cb-5fc9-800c-1394bd01e241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491649} {:answer/id #uuid "7873082b-15f9-5724-b785-35989be3f136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491664}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491595} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa4fd8bc-59fc-526b-b28d-cb3ea2256c56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6397dc6a-e670-5a27-9059-a00e14d9ffee" :question/math-academy-id "q-94024" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e1bb2cd0-ae3d-5129-96c2-1f14197550c9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555904}}] :activity/title "Diagonalization of 2x2 Symmetric Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554995}]}
